==Romanian==
{{wikipedia|lang=ro}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|ro|Молдова|sc=Cyrl||post-1930s Cyrillic form}}

===Etymology===
Of unclear origin. The suffix ''-ova'' is probably of {{der|ro|sla|-}} origin. Theories about the first element:
* It is possibly ultimately from {{der|ro|got|𐌼𐌿𐌻𐌳𐌰||dust, mud}} (possibly [[w:Moldavia|Moldavia]]); compare the river {{m|da|Mulde}} in Germany and {{m|de|Moldau}}, the German name of the river {{m|en|Vltava}}, which may be of related origin.
* Alternatively, it may be related to {{cog|ro|molid||spruce}}, though the loss of the ''i'' in this word is difficult to explain.
* A folk etymology, according to [[w:Dimitrie Cantemir|Dimitrie Cantemir]], says the name of the [[w:Moldova River|Moldova River]] comes from Prince [[w:Dragoș|Dragoș]], after his hound ''Molda'', who drowned in it while attempting to hunt an aurochs.
* The Romanian people may have called themselves "Moldoveni" or "Munteni" (literally "of the mountains") so Moldova may alternatively derive from a {{der|ro|txh|-}}-{{der|ro|xil|-}} language, from {{m|xyl|*mol||mountain}} + {{m|txh|*dava||town}}.<ref>Ștefan Pascu, Documente străine despre români, ed. Arhivelor statului, București 1992, {{ISBN|973-95711-2-3}}</ref>
For more theories see {{pedia|Name of Moldova|Name of Moldova|i=1}}

